#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083c5ede-cedd-556a-8b65-fe48a6829892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500481} {:answer/id #uuid "119acc6e-65cb-58c0-bc0c-21f9dc698553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500505} {:answer/id #uuid "96e02c89-8c88-58ca-a757-3724f4c8eaae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500775} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcca706-e138-501f-b9cf-da409da5c70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500865} {:answer/id #uuid "d6603e3d-1da4-5eb2-b8fd-71573abd6fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96e02c89-8c88-58ca-a757-3724f4c8eaae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500775} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bb54f63-1362-5d2e-8c6a-5e8e2485a25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f7aceb3-5a6a-584b-8488-47f8fb82e9a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125649" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f178c33254644c5e2e7bf6f3c596df87b1ea2f0b5bfd417c3f279f6c62b1b0d.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f87d2e735ad58e0b33a813fb91ab202ac0eeaf90575979ea45f954fb7e1d0493.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/10/102c11f9002e29a3759679c6ff0bd10188908d33eb9cc94a84d900956adc33a5.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36470539-f464-546f-ba73-6270b016995f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435296} {:answer/id #uuid "39a9b6f2-d460-516e-a343-dad430e857a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435297} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef092e1-14dd-5d66-a852-0395cd09a641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435298} {:answer/id #uuid "5fffa88a-ab60-5e5a-ae9d-85cd50c46069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435301} {:answer/id #uuid "f7609979-80a6-5ca8-82ec-72fc4cf0ed29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7609979-80a6-5ca8-82ec-72fc4cf0ed29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435307} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0bec69d-d68a-518d-bb91-2fd951e08467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4ffdafd-ca0f-5aeb-9afa-3ab07c6f0225" :question/math-academy-id "q-143426" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{1000}$ is a sample of size $n=1\\,000$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =150$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=25,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=25$ and the sample size is $n=1\\,000,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{25}{1\\,000} \\\\  & =0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.025}≈0.16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb3b4b6a-3a24-5079-bf33-ed1c5576ffa7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b405321-a053-5f3f-aa87-24ec0e736170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159546} {:answer/id #uuid "61b79666-a6d5-539f-81d1-2e3b56449d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500667} {:answer/id #uuid "70d54670-c738-50bc-afb0-0d6374b4b4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500698} {:answer/id #uuid "c42b7b33-0490-5c36-8f01-54cc20ab39d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500879} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc661d-e145-5e66-b9b9-6631ae091f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc661d-e145-5e66-b9b9-6631ae091f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500990} :answer-field/id #uuid "968220d8-4854-5451-a29d-7b23b30726b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d20c523-ad94-5c8f-adf5-6fe709391a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-143459" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\,0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{x-1}^{1-x}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y\\,{|}_{x-1}^{1-x} \\\\  & =(1-x)-(x-1) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2x, & \\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2-2x}, & \\,0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b46ce4-cfe0-5da6-bc91-65ae8c544393" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Through a Surface as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "620afa66-2c6c-57b7-a3ae-d62a79f7f8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500671} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebe624d-df33-5b1a-ac75-393b05dce501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500692} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd3bc80-2dcb-5963-9f05-c0a968725eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}(3u+2v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500784} {:answer/id #uuid "c2689dcd-d1d7-54f8-a7ca-f907d83e79ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}(uv+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500873} {:answer/id #uuid "f99ddd32-d363-5197-81ba-b8f06c983f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{2}{v}^{2}(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "620afa66-2c6c-57b7-a3ae-d62a79f7f8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500671} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fefe9c8-5fc6-5bbb-bfe2-94890c45b24c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2343ccc6-302e-57d1-903e-91d537e6ff5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161385" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,\\,-2y,\\,-z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{3},\\,{v}^{3},\\,uv⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨-2x,\\,-2y,\\,-z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{3}\\,y={v}^{3}\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨-2{u}^{3},\\,-2{v}^{3},\\,-uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-2{u}^{3},\\,-2{v}^{3},\\,-uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =(-2{u}^{3})\\cdot (-3{v}^{3})+(-2{v}^{3})\\cdot (-3{u}^{3})+(-uv)\\cdot 9{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}+6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}-9{u}^{3}{v}^{3} \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b2c4733-27b9-52be-b326-b4360580b685" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of a Rod About the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f03b339-985e-5e23-a739-bca6ba574cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500530} {:answer/id #uuid "3c41b729-6e05-5636-8a3b-1a37ee53810d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500587} {:answer/id #uuid "77f963e0-0ec4-566b-93f6-f6ae435001d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500716} {:answer/id #uuid "7fed4cfe-7a06-52f7-99ff-25232d7ce97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500732} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9d062d-1816-571e-bbf1-1777667627d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c41b729-6e05-5636-8a3b-1a37ee53810d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500587} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfbf2f05-cb8e-50e3-add0-d21c0e98ca91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41337cdf-5450-5e5f-ba7a-c0f13d6a2b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-163038" :question/problem "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=5\\sqrt{x},$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 1,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=5\\sqrt{x},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 5\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27693351-84ba-5cf8-944f-20cf97e35295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{c}_{1}t}+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{c}_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282028} {:answer/id #uuid "55c460b4-b90c-5153-8760-e9dcd6e57a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,c\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282417} {:answer/id #uuid "87502e7f-7f1b-5cd5-8388-3373709410f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282856} {:answer/id #uuid "b2930186-51d4-5ada-b0f3-1488d79396f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283237} {:answer/id #uuid "de395909-c010-5007-ac35-db1d40fcbed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=c[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,c\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87502e7f-7f1b-5cd5-8388-3373709410f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282856} :answer-field/id #uuid "af38cfdc-f9e4-5281-957c-90a87b6ede28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88fe7310-fc98-5239-8ffe-c165e6875721" :question/math-academy-id "q-168497" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccbd978-8163-5c7e-baae-b8ad95985a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342350} {:answer/id #uuid "453e49ec-25af-50ef-847c-f9e8f100fa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342807} {:answer/id #uuid "78d2918a-06f4-5e2f-8f95-8ce2e0a1d53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343281} {:answer/id #uuid "7c924cb2-9540-5923-aef1-54902d61b6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343312} {:answer/id #uuid "a953ff99-deba-55f2-9b54-5b5b5650e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ccbd978-8163-5c7e-baae-b8ad95985a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342350} :answer-field/id #uuid "10c5cc9a-ab7f-5bd2-8f67-b0bc3b91ae44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fc56ee5-fc5d-5429-bfdb-4254f7ee30fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-77191" :question/problem "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\frac{3}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨-2,0⟩+t(⟨0,1⟩-⟨-2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨-2+2t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(-2+2t)i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=-2+2t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(x-2y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j \\\\  & =((-2+2t)-2t)\\,i+(t-(-2+2t))\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(-2\\,i+(2-t)\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =-2-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2-t)\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={[-2t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =[-2(1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0]-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac6ffda-9fff-5f00-9079-1971793890a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=5(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500551} {:answer/id #uuid "3aaf6df3-4c0c-5ce2-91b9-1f3eba10496a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500585} {:answer/id #uuid "559434b9-9a2d-5235-9416-53044593424a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500640} {:answer/id #uuid "e99681fa-8480-528a-936e-a22041e85c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500968} {:answer/id #uuid "f94777e9-96eb-57dd-a363-17a5520ac6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f94777e9-96eb-57dd-a363-17a5520ac6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500998} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a88d5f4-d750-5f16-a3bb-b2420ae3e78d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9927cf9d-99cc-5df6-888f-210e0aad32bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-97540" :question/problem "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $2\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(5,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(5,0),$ we have that $r(0)=5i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=5.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=5$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
